name ar-arena
time 2026-07-27T12:00:00Z
carry units ground stores
stop_on never

# THE ARENA — THIRTY MEASURING RUNGS FOR THE DOCTRINE EVOLUTION. NOT A CAMPAIGN, NOT A HISTORY.
# Spec, grid and the whole derivation: doc/campaigns/ar-arena.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT ONE OF THE TEN CAMPAIGNS OF doc/campaigns/INDEX.md AND MUST NOT BE READ AS ONE. Those
# ten are scenario specifications with cited anchors, and INDEX.md's own sentence — "a campaign without a
# cited anchor is a mood, and a mood cannot be measured" — is exactly why this file declares up front
# that it HAS no anchor and is not trying to have one. It is an INSTRUMENT: thirty synthetic cells built
# to the four properties `E11` of doc/doctrine-evolution.md measured a cell needs before it can be
# informative, and to nothing else.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS, IN ONE MEASUREMENT. `E11` §7, flown over all 154 committed campaign cells with 24
# levers: 44 cells put ≥ 4 aircraft on the graded side, 36 of those are opposed, and exactly ONE reaches
# S2's threshold of 8 movers — and that one flips 1 of 8 on S7's spawn grid. S5 wants three informative
# cells; the campaign breadth supplies zero. §8 then locates the cause and says it is not a defect of any
# campaign: every campaign is a LADDER, 107 of 154 cells are its lower rungs at one or two aircraft, and
# ten ladders produce only ten tops. This file adds rungs that are all top — and it adds them BESIDE
# the campaigns rather than inside them, because editing a committed rung to raise a mover count is
# selecting the arena on the result, which doc/doctrine-evolution.md has declined five times.
#
# THE FOUR PROPERTIES, AND EVERY RUNG CARRIES ALL FOUR (each rung's own header repeats them with its own
# numbers):
#   1 LARGE    ≥ 4 F-16 and ≥ 4 MiG-29, always in flights of FOUR — four seats deep because `aftM =
#              element × trail` with `element = (position−1)/2`, so two of G1's six levers are
#              structurally dead on a pair (`E11` §4)
#   2 OPPOSED  MiG-29s under a GCI brief on the graded hostile team, armed with a BVR round or without
#              one — but never absent
#   3 VISIBLE  `survive` on every aircraft plus at least one contested `kill unit <named>` — never
#              `kill team`, which on these files would need every ground unit dead as well and could
#              therefore never be met
#   4 ROBUST   the baseline outcome class over `fb_campaign_arena.py`'s own ±3 m spawn grid. Rungs
#              01…10 publish it per rung; rungs 11…30 deliberately do NOT measure it, because S7 is the
#              GATE's screen and a rung built until its own baseline stops moving is a rung selected on
#              its own result
#
# WHAT THE FIRST TEN SPAN — §4.2's FIRST axis, initial aspect: five entry geometries × ten distinct
# environments (clock and atmosphere):
#   01 headon-noon        A head-on   12:00Z  sun +64.16   calm
#   02 headon-night       A head-on   00:30Z  sun −25.59   wind 335/19.6 kt (the fixture's 6 000 m wind)
#   03 offset-morning     B offset    08:00Z  sun +33.34   GFS fixture (96 % overcast)
#   04 offset-night       B offset    22:00Z  sun −19.83   calm
#   05 beam-dawn          C beam      04:40Z  sun −0.81    wind 315/15.9 kt (the fixture's 3 000 m wind)
#   06 beam-afternoon     C beam      16:30Z  sun +30.30   GFS fixture
#   07 close-dusk         D close     19:20Z  sun +1.15    calm
#   08 close-day          D close     10:00Z  sun +53.23   GFS fixture
#   09 vertical-predawn   E vertical  03:00Z  sun −14.68   wind 359.6/25.6 kt (the fixture's 9 000 m wind)
#   10 vertical-evening   E vertical  18:00Z  sun +14.48   calm
# The sun elevations are MEASURED, off each run's own `mission CLOCK` line at the primary actor's spawn
# point; the range sits on the prime meridian so UTC IS local apparent solar time and the clock column
# is checkable by eye. NO RUNG IS A CONTROL OF ANY OTHER — two coordinates differ between any pair — and
# the set is a SPREAD, not a factorial. It exists to give the evolution thirty independent cells, never
# to attribute an effect to daylight, and a reader who reads a light/weather effect out of two rungs is
# reading a geometry change as well.
#
# WHAT THE SECOND TEN SPAN — the THREE axes of §4.2 the first ten do not touch, each named there with the
# measurement that says it changes the answer: ENERGY ASYMMETRY (`split` inverts the whole ranking),
# FORCE RATIO (the cooperative channel is worth an outcome band on `split` and nothing on `mirror`) and
# WEAPON OBLIGATION (0.3 s of binding against 17.3 s, a factor of 58). The two energy seats and the
# co-altitude reference are the tree's OWN, lifted from `tools/fb_tournament.py`'s GEOMETRIES rather than
# invented: `mirror` = 8 000 m / 420 kt on both sides, `split` = 12 000 m / 500 kt against 6 000 m /
# 350 kt, i.e. verbatim §4.2's "co-alt/co-speed vs 6,000 m + 150 kt". The force ratio is varied by adding
# and removing FLIGHTS of four, never by thinning a side to a pair, so property 1 survives every cell.
# Weapon binding is set by the ROUND alone — AIM-120/R-73 free (`FBSeekerHandoverS` ≥ 0), AIM-7/R-27R
# bound to impact (−1) — with radar, fuel and countermeasures untouched:
#   #  rung                geom     blue:red  energy          weapon blue/red   clock   sun      wx
#   11 energy-blue-high    A head-on   8:8    BLUE 12 km/500   free / bound     06:15Z  +14.88   calm
#   12 energy-red-high     A head-on   8:8    RED  12 km/500   free / bound     13:45Z  +57.08   GFS fixture
#   13 energy-flat         A head-on   8:8    co-alt/co-speed  free / bound     20:45Z  −11.30   wind 352.8/23.2
#   14 ratio-two-one       B offset    8:4    co-alt/co-speed  BOUND / bound    09:20Z  +47.02   calm
#   15 ratio-one-two       B offset    8:16   co-alt/co-speed  free / bound     01:50Z  −21.64   GFS fixture
#   16 ratio-three-one     B offset   12:4    co-alt/co-speed  free / bound     15:10Z  +44.19   wind 301.1/16.6
#   17 ratio-two-three     C beam      8:12   RED  12 km/500   free / FREE      23:40Z  −25.75   GFS fixture
#   18 bind-both           D close     8:8    co-alt/co-speed  BOUND / bound    11:40Z  +63.62   calm
#   19 bind-neither        D close     8:8    co-alt/co-speed  free / FREE      05:20Z   +5.59   calm
#   20 bind-blue-high      C beam      8:4    BLUE 12 km/500   BOUND / FREE     17:05Z  +24.11   wind 9.2/30.9
# Each axis appears BOTH alone — 11/12/13 move only the energy, 14/15/16 only the ratio, 18/19 only the
# binding — and crossed with another (14, 17, 20), so no axis is confounded with a second one. The three
# closed-form winds are the committed GFS fixture's own wind at the altitude each rung's measurement
# lives in, measured with `build/fb-test-weather` and restated as a closed form, which is the rule rungs
# 02/05/09 already use.
#
# WHAT THE THIRD TEN SPAN — NOTHING NEW, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Rungs 21…30 add no axis; they fill the
# four the arena already spans DENSER, in (aspect, force, energy, weapon) tuples that appear in no rung
# of 01…20, and each carries a clock that appears in none either. The reason is a measurement rather
# than a taste: [MESS, the gate over all 40 cells of rungs 01…20, tree 5866aae] TWO cells are informative
# — `ar-08-close-day:f16` at 9 movers and `ar-15-ratio-one-two:f16` at 8, both at 0 flips of 8 — and the
# yield is 5 % of cells in BOTH tens. S5 wants three. At a stable 5 % the remaining question is a COUNT,
# not a lever, and the honest way to answer a count is to add cells built to the same rule.
#   #  rung                geom          b:r  energy            weapon blue/red clock      sun  wx
#   21 headon-two-three    A head-on    8:12  co-alt/co-speed   free / FREE    07:10Z  +24.51  calm
#   22 headon-three-two    A head-on    12:8  RED  12 km/500    BOUND / bound  14:20Z  +52.18  wind 9.2/30.9
#   23 offset-blue-high    B offset      8:8  BLUE 12 km/500    BOUND / FREE   21:15Z  −15.05  GFS fixture
#   24 offset-three-three  B offset    12:12  co-alt/co-speed   free / bound   02:25Z  −18.51  calm
#   25 beam-red-high       C beam        8:8  RED  12 km/500    BOUND / bound  12:50Z  +62.65  wind 335/19.6
#   26 beam-three-two      C beam       12:8  co-alt/co-speed   free / bound   18:40Z   +7.67  GFS fixture
#   27 close-blue-high     D close      8:12  BLUE 12 km/500    free / bound   00:05Z  −25.81  GFS fixture
#   28 close-two-one       D close       8:4  co-alt/co-speed   free / FREE    10:45Z  +59.15  calm
#   29 vertical-two-one    E vertical    8:4  stack 11…11.5 km  BOUND / bound  16:00Z  +35.59  calm
#   30 vertical-two-three  E vertical   8:12  stack 9…11.5 km   free / FREE    03:55Z   −7.50  wind 5.1/28.3
# Two ratios here exist nowhere in 01…20 — 12:8 (`ar-22`, `ar-26`) and 12:12 (`ar-24`) — so the force
# axis now carries SEVEN distinct ratios instead of five. Geometry E is carried into this ten at two force
# ratios, and its stack is a FORCE coordinate acting inside the geometry: rungs 09/10 stack two flights
# at 10…11.5 km, `ar-29` stacks one at 11…11.5 and `ar-30` three at 9…11.5, one shelf per flight.
# THE FIFTH AXIS OF §4.2 IS STILL NOT SPANNED, and no rung of this file pretends otherwise: DETECTION
# SYMMETRY is a CONSTANT of all thirty — red `n019 rad` at 54 nm, blue `crm` at 40 nm, in every file.
# Spanning it is a fourth ten, not a denser third.
# AND THE SAFETY CATCH: rungs 21…30 are built to the rule and NOT toward a mover count. No rung of this
# arena is edited because it fails and none is removed; the stand grows monotonically, and WHICH cells
# are informative is the gate's OUTPUT and never its input.
#
# ONE THING RUNGS 11…30 DO THAT RUNGS 01…10 DO NOT, and it is a correction rather than a variation: their
# `kill unit` assignment COVERS EVERY AIRCRAFT ON BOTH SIDES — once, or twice where the naming side is
# the smaller one, but never zero times. doc/missions/verdict.md's
# expected-loss rule makes a loss decisive unless another unit declared a `kill` naming it, and a
# decisive loss ENDS the run — [MESS, the first pass of the second ten under the partial assignment of
# rungs 01…10] `ar-16` exited 1 truncated at t = 149.2 s and `ar-19` exited 1 at t = 99.4 s, each on one
# aircraft nobody had named, so the run LENGTH and with it the ground half's reachability depended on
# WHICH aircraft happened to die. Under complete coverage all twenty end in TIMEOUT by construction.
#
# EVERY RUNG COMBINES AIR AND GROUND. There is no air-only and no ground-only cell: four `target_soft`
# aim points (one graded objective each, on the four strikers), two `target_hard` present and
# deliberately ungraded (their Mk 84 fail radius is 17.7 m against a delivery this tree lands at
# 32…87 m — a graded objective there would be a coin), a P-18 node, an SA-6 and a ZSU-23-4 under one net.
#
# THE CARRY IS DECLARED AND INERT, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `carry units ground stores` is the default and
# is written out rather than narrowed, but every callsign is prefixed with its own rung (`ar01*` …
# `ar30*`), so the thirty casts are PAIRWISE DISJOINT in every unit they can lose and the overlay has
# nothing to apply. Consequence, and it is what an arena needs: each rung is measurable STANDALONE, the
# gate flies them one at a time, and campaign step k is byte-identical to its own standalone run —
# doc/missions/campaign.md §5 criterion 2 holds by construction instead of by measurement. `stop_on
# never` for the same reason: a rung that ends badly must not delete the measurements behind it.
#
# READING RULE FOR THE WHOLE FILE, BINDING. THE CAMPAIGN EXIT CODE IS NOT A VERDICT and neither is any
# rung's. Every rung is a measuring rig whose normal end is TIMEOUT (exit 3), and the campaign exit is
# the worst step's. THE MEASUREMENT IS THE PAIR (V, M) PER GRADED SIDE PER RUNG, computed as
# doc/doctrine-evolution.md §1 computes it: V = Σ of the judge's `UNIT_RESULT` ranks over the side's
# aircraft (SUCCESS 3, TIMEOUT/NONE 2, FAIL 1, CRASH/LOC 0), M = the count of that side's
# `mission OBJECTIVE … state=met` lines. Read, per rung and in this order: (1) both sides' `mission
# OBJECTIVE` blocks — a side that publishes none has lost property 3 and nothing else in that rung is
# worth reading; (2) `stores DELIVERY aimErrM` against the 100.2 m soft fail radius; (3) `site LAUNCH …
# brgDeg`, because a FlightBox battery has no IFF interrogator and WHO it shot at is a result; (4) the
# (V, M) pair. Do NOT read a kill count as a lever's effect.

